      "text": "   Let $x$ be Jane's current age.\n\nWe can set up an equation involving perfect squares and perfect cubes.\n\nWe know that one year ago, Jane's age was $x-1$ years, a perfect square. Therefore, we can write:\n\n$x-1 = m^{2}$, where $m$ is a positive integer.\n\nAfter one year, Jane's age will be $x+1$ years. We know that $x+1$ will be a perfect cube. Therefore, we can write:\n\n$x+1 = n^{3}$, where $n$ is a positive integer.\n\nNow, we can solve the equation $m^{2} = x-1$ for $x$ in terms of $m$:\n\n$x = 1 + m^{2}$.\n\nSubstitute this expression for $x$ into the equation $x+1 = n^{3}$:\n\n$1 + m^{2} + 1 = n^{3}$, or $2 + m^{2} = n^{3}$.\n\nThe only small or medium-sized value of $m$ that satisfies this equation is $m = 2$. For this value of $m$, we can calculate:\n\n$2 + m^{2} = n^{3}$, $2 + 2^{2} = n^{3}$, $2 + 4 = n^{3}$, $6 = n^{3}$.\n\nHowever, $6$ is not a perfect cube, so this value of $m$ cannot be Jane's age. Therefore, there is no solution for this problem. There is no Jane who is that age and this age difference that works.",
